# --threadsafe on a NON-PASCAL frontend. This combination had NEVER been
# executed by any gate on any box, which is how `--threadsafe` shipped in the
# pin hanging or segfaulting on every single NilPy program, down to a one-line
# print (bug-a-threadsafe-segfaults-on-every-nilpy-program).
#
# Two independent defects had to line up to produce that, and this file is
# written to catch either one coming back:
#
#   1. only the Pascal driver emitted the I/O-lock stubs, so IOLockAddr stayed
#      0 and every write called code offset 0 -- the ELF ENTRY POINT -- which
#      jumps back to main, re-mmaps the arena and pushes a return address, until
#      the 8 MB stack is gone. A plain `print` catches that.
#
#   2. BSS_IO_OWNER and BSS_IO_DEPTH were likewise allocated only by the Pascal
#      driver, so both stayed 0 and the owner tid and the depth counter WERE THE
#      SAME EIGHT BYTES. `inc [depth]` scribbled on the owner, nothing could
#      ever win the cmpxchg, and the acquire stub spun forever. That one only
#      shows up once the depth counter is actually USED -- i.e. when the lock is
#      taken re-entrantly -- which is why the nested case below is not optional
#      decoration. A single flat print would have passed with the fields still
#      aliased.
#
# The lock is REENTRANT by design: a write argument may call a function that
# itself writes, and that must not self-deadlock.

def emit(n):
    # writes while the caller's write statement already holds the I/O lock ->
    # depth goes to 2 and back. This is the case that needs owner and depth to
    # be distinct storage.
    print("inner", n)
    return n * 2


def outer():
    # the argument evaluation writes, then the enclosing print writes
    print("outer", emit(3))


print("hi")
outer()

total = 0
for i in range(3):
    total = total + emit(i)
print("total", total)
